**Start with round robin.** It works for 80% of use cases. If your backend servers are the same hardware and your requests take roughly the same time (typical REST CRUD), round robin distributes load evenly with zero overhead.

**Switch to least connections when request durations vary.** If your service handles both 5ms GET requests and 30-second file uploads, round robin will overload servers that get clustered file uploads. Least connections naturally routes new requests to servers that have finished their previous work.

**Switch to consistent hashing when you need key-based routing.** Caching layers (Memcached, Redis cluster) need the same key to hit the same server so the cache is effective. Sharded databases need deterministic routing. Consistent hashing provides this while minimizing redistribution when servers join or leave.

**Use weighted variants when hardware is mixed.** During rolling upgrades or when your fleet has different instance types, weighted round robin or weighted least connections prevent overloading smaller machines.

```java
// Spring Cloud LoadBalancer — custom algorithm example
@Bean
public ReactorLoadBalancer<ServiceInstance> leastConnectionsLB(
        ObjectProvider<ServiceInstanceListSupplier> provider) {
    return new LeastConnectionsLoadBalancer(provider);
}

// Nginx equivalent:
// upstream backend {
//     least_conn;              // least connections
//     server app1:8080 weight=3;  // weighted: 3× traffic
//     server app2:8080 weight=1;
// }
```

**Health checks are non-negotiable.** Every load balancer must actively probe backends. Passive health checks (marking a server unhealthy after N failed requests) are too slow — a 30-second detection delay at 1K req/sec means 30K failed requests. Active checks (HTTP GET `/health` every 5 seconds) catch failures before users do.
